			<div class="content" id="post-content-3924761">
									

<p>&quot;Before we knew how to farm, before we lived in villages, before we even knew how to make pots, we built a star temple on a hill. The simplicity of this statement belies its astounding implications. […] We did not build Göbekli Tepe in Southeastern Turkey because a surplus of stored food allowed a priestly class to emerge and tell us make-believe stories. We did not monitor the movements of the stars because it told us when to plant crops, because we weren’t planting crops.We built Göbekli Tepe because it expressed something about ourselves and our place in the universe. The temple complex restores the quest for meaning to its preeminent place atop the goals of human life. Philosophy is rescued from being a mere nighttime hobby of farmers to being the defining human trait, that which we value most. This is what the site’s discoverer, Dr Klaus Schmidt, means when he says that the cathedral predates the city. It may well have been the coming together for spiritual reasons that birthed settled cultures, rather than the other way around.&quot;<br />
-<em>Star.Ships: A Prehistory Of The Spirits, Gordon White</em></p>
<p>This was a long piece to finish. I think I had the idea basically in 2016? The earliest concept of this didn't involve Gamers Against Weed at all and would not have been remotely this long, or involving the same anomaly (in all but one aspect). Some things changed steadily over time as I'd set aside the concept, do a little work, and rethink how I wanted to approach this. After one particularly heavy reformatting following submitting a 'just finished enough to be at least looked at' draft to the drafts forum for critique I found myself looking at something that now had the basis for being something I really liked, but that would need far more to be completed. I chose at that time to leave writing it alone for a while so as not to burn out. That continued for a significant period of time (which was all very fine and lovely time; I was entirely pleased to leave an unfinished draft in my sandbox and do unrelated activities for a period of several months) until I, inspired by some of the things <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dolphinslugchugger" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1969477); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1969477&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1535625767" alt="DolphinSlugchugger" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1969477)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dolphinslugchugger" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1969477); return false;">DolphinSlugchugger</a></span> has done writing and reinterpreting other works, asked it if it would like to co-author this piece with me. It agreed, and steady progress resumed, uninterrupted by the feeling of having hit a wall with what I had just written myself into (sometimes interrupted by video games) and with <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dolphinslugchugger" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1969477); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1969477&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1535625767" alt="DolphinSlugchugger" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1969477)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dolphinslugchugger" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1969477); return false;">DolphinSlugchugger</a></span>'s excellent and significant work I feel we were together able to write an article we can both be proud of.</p>
<p>I have a lot of people to thank, and this post is already of significant length, so that's all going under a collapsible.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
